/*
  core/stressors/alert_volume.spthy -- f_U for sigma9 AlertVolume / DecisionFatigue, CEREMONY-AGNOSTIC.

  Migrated to the agnostic interface (AGNOSTIC_STRESSOR_INTERFACE.md). This is a DENSITY detector, not
  a per-step workload-level lookup, so it does not read the lexicon. The old rule (file
  alert_volume_inferred.spthy) counted handled-decision markers -- three distinct !Did. This version
  counts the OBJECTIVE decision steps the ceremony presents -- three distinct !Step(P,_,'decide') --
  so it reads the SAME Layer-1 vocabulary as every other migrated detector and names no ceremony fact.
  'decide' is a generic interaction-taxonomy category (choose among options), not a task name.

  Alert / alarm fatigue (Cvach 2012; MFA prompt-bombing): >= k decision steps in one ceremony fatigues
  the user into disengagement -> SetMask Careless. The THRESHOLD k = 3 is the HCI parameter (the design
  doc's "named parameter, not rule arity"); Tamarin counts distinct facts by arity, so k is realised as
  three !Step premises + Neq distinctness, documented here -- bump k by adding/removing a premise.
  Bounded once per party (OneAlertVolume) so the count reasoning terminates.

  NB: counting is at PRESENT time (the step is shown), not handle time -- fatigue from being shown the
  alerts. The old !Did count was at handle time; no lemma distinguishes, and SetMask precedence
  (S1_degrade_from_interface_step) is preserved because the decision producer's UIStep precedes the
  !Step facts. Inequality must NOT be combined in one theory with sigma8's identically-named restriction
  (kept apart: sigma8 in P2/P6, sigma9 in P4/S1).
*/

rule Detect_AlertVolume [color=#eb5757]:
    [ !Step(P, r1, 'decide'), !Step(P, r2, 'decide'), !Step(P, r3, 'decide'), !StressEnable(P) ]
  --[ AlertVolume(P), Neq(r1,r2), Neq(r1,r3), Neq(r2,r3), SetMask(P,'Careless') ]->
    [ ]

restriction Inequality:
  "All x #i. Neq(x,x)@i ==> F"

restriction OneAlertVolume:
  "All p #i #j. AlertVolume(p)@i & AlertVolume(p)@j ==> #i = #j"
